    <!-- formatted="false" because the four literal percent signs read to aapt as four
         non-positional format specifiers. Without it every build warns in all twelve
         locales, and the day someone gives this string an argument String.format throws
         UnknownFormatConversionException on " t" instead of printing the sentence. The
         signs must stay literal: this is retrieved with no arguments, so escaping them as
         %% would put "%%" on screen rather than "%".

         The same attribute is on fin_rate, fin_tip_percent, fin_discount_1, fin_discount_2
         and fin_tax_rate, in all twelve locales, for the same reason. They each end in a
         single literal "%", which aapt tolerates silently — one non-positional specifier is
         not the "multiple substitutions" case it warns about — so they went years without
         it while the string with four signs was documented. That asymmetry is the trap: a
         label that ends in a bare % and is later handed a value ("Annual rate 7.5 %") dies
         in composition with UnknownFormatConversionException naming a stray character
         rather than the resource. Any new label ending in a literal % gets the attribute
         at the moment it is written. -->
    <string name="fin_successive_note" formatted="false">Successive discounts compose: 20% then 10% is 28% off, not 30%.</string>
